"Discrete Applied Mathematics 284 (2020) 42–52 (https://perso.limos.fr/ffoucaud/Publications/Papers/J32_LD-digraphs_DAM_2020.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Domination and location in twin-free digraphs ... Received in revised form 20 February 2020 ... in graph theory: see the two classic books [10,11] on the subject.\nDiscrete Applied Mathematics 284 (2020) 42–52\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect\nDiscrete Applied Mathematics\njournal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/dam\nDomination and location in twin-free digraphs\nFlorent Foucaud a,b,1, Shahrzad Heydarshahi b,∗, Aline Parreau c\na Univ. Bordeaux, Bordeaux INP, CNRS, LaBRI, UMR5800, F-33400 Talence, France\nb Univ. Orléans, INSA Centre Val de Loire, LIFO EA 4022, F-45067 Orléans Cedex 2, France\nc Univ Lyon, Université Claude Bernard, CNRS, LIRIS - UMR 5205, F69622, France\na r t i c l e\ni n f o\na b s t r a c t\nArticle history:\nReceived 14 December 2018\nReceived in revised form 20 February 2020\nAccepted 4 March 2020\nAvailable online 12 March 2020\nKeywords:\nDigraphs\nDominating sets\nLocating-dominating sets\nBounds\nA dominating set D in a digraph is a set of vertices such that every vertex is either in D or\nhas an in-neighbour in D. A dominating set D of a digraph is locating-dominating if every\nvertex not in D has a unique set of in-neighbours within D. The location-domination\nThe\ndomination number γ (G) of G is the smallest size of a dominating set of G. The area of domination is one of the main topics\nin graph theory: see the two classic books [10,11] on the subject. While there are hundreds of papers on domination\nin undirected graphs, domination in digraphs is less studied; we refer to the papers [8,14,15] for some examples. One\nparticular variation of domination is the concept of location-domination, introduced by Slater for undirected graphs in [19]\n(see also [17,18]). For a set S of vertices of a digraph G, two vertices x and y of V(G)\\ S are located by S if there is a vertex\nof S that is an in-neighbour of exactly one vertex among x and y. The set S is a locating set of G if it locates all the\npairs of V(G) \\ S (but does not necessarily dominate the graph). Equivalently, every vertex not in S has a distinct set of\nin-neighbours in S. The location number of a graph G is the size of a smallest locating set and is denoted by loc(G). A set\nD of vertices of a digraph G is locating-dominating if it is both locating and dominating. The location-domination number\nγL(G) of a digraph G is the smallest size of a locating-dominating set of G. Note that γL(G) −1 ≤loc(G) ≤γL(G), since at\nmost one vertex is not dominated in a locating set.\n∗Corresponding author.\nE-mail addresses:\nflorent.foucaud@gmail.fr (F. Foucaud), shahrzad.heydarshahi@univ-orleans.fr (S. Heydarshahi), aline.parreau@univ-lyon1.fr\n(A. Parreau).\n1 This author is partially supported by the ANR project HOSIGRA (ANR-17-CE40-0022) and the IFCAM, India project ‘‘Applications of graph\nhomomorphisms’’ (MA/IFCAM/18/39).\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2020.03.025\n0166-218X/© 2020 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDomination in Digraphs - University of Johannesburg (https://pure.uj.ac.za/en/publications/domination-in-digraphs/)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; Research output: Chapter in Book/Report/Conference proceeding › Chapter › peer-review ... @inbook{72391b7f49224609bf13a102bfa5c0a3, ... Haynes, TW, Hedetniemi, ST & Henning, MA 2021, Domination in Digraphs. in Developments in Mathematics.\n\n# Domination in Digraphs\n\n  * Teresa W. Haynes\n  * , Stephen T. Hedetniemi\n  * , Michael A. Henning\n\n  * Mathematics and Applied Mathematics\n\n  * East Tennessee State University\n  * University of Johannesburg\n  * Clemson University College of Engineering, Computing and Applied Sciences\n\nResearch output: Chapte